    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d241">

                <pb facs="0415" n="1873" xml:id="pg1873"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">293.1111/12–1951: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cumming</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-12-19T15:00:00+03:00">December 19,
                            1951—3 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1050. Re mytel 1021, December 14.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d241-f1"/> This afternoon
                    gave Gromyko <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> re treatment Amers by Chi
                    Commie authorities (Deptel 410, Dec 13). Gromyko said he could add nothing to
                    what had been previously said by Vyshinsky; Sov Govt had no responsibility for
                    US citizens in Chi; matter was solely one between US and Chi Peoples Govt;
                    latter sitn not changed by fact US had no diplomatic rels Peking. With somewhat
                    grimly humorous smile he added that as regards the Amer who was said to have
                    died in prison “people could die in their beds or anywhere else”. I commented
                    that death in bed was normal; death in prison was not.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cumming</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d241-f1" n="1" xml:id="d241-f1-content">
                <p>The reference telegram stated that Gumming saw no objection to taking up with
                    Gromyko the question of the treatment of Americans by the Chinese Communists
                    (293.1111/12–1251).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
